apple and nvidia are going to war over  the claims for having the world's  fastest computer gpu and the craziest  part is that they're using two  completely different methods to get it  done with apple going all out on  performance per watt with their 128 core  m1 max quadra gpu while nvidia is  literally just throwing metric tons of  power at its upcoming rtx 4090 as i'll  show you in just a minute and i'm also  going to be covering a wide range of  topics like chip shortages and how they  could be getting a lot worse for pc  buyers while apple is actually in a  really good position with their upcoming  multi-die chiplet designs and then we've  got to discuss the brand new performance  and price leaks for nvidia's rtx 4000  series and then finally why buying a  high-end mac might be a more  cost-effective choice if you need insane  levels of graphics performance so let's  get right into it just the other day a  digit times report came out with brand  new details about the upcoming rtx 4000  ada lovelace gpus that are extremely  positive from a performance point of  view and that comes after a bunch of  other details that have already come out  but before we dig into what all of this  could mean for these new nvidia gpus  we've got to discuss apple's major plans  mark german who was the most reliable  apple leaker out there has already  confirmed that apple is working on much  more powerful chips with two and four  times the number of cpu and gpu cores  than their current m1 max chip and the  way they're going to do this is by  literally taking those ammo max dies and  combining them together into a single  system-on-chip package by using some  form of high bandwidth infinity fabric  to connect them all together to minimize  efficiency loss and in one of my  previous videos i actually estimated the  performance of the four die m1 max  quadro chip in terms of the cross  platform 3d mark wildlife extreme  benchmark which simulates gaming  performance and the new chip  outperformed the absolute best record  score ever which was an insanely  overclocked rtx 3090 but when you look  at the 30 90 cards with an out of the  box overclock of around 1.8 gigahertz  the m1 max quadra is now around 63  faster than the 3090 which is super  impressive and if you're curious about  how i did those calculations i took the  32 core m1 max which already has  basically perfect four times scaling  compared to the eight core and one chip  and then i multiplied it by four but  then i took out ten percent to account  for some efficiency loss since the four  dice have to communicate between each  other which could add in a bit of  latency however there is a chance that  we might not even see a 10 efficiency  loss because of tsmc's very impressive  kawas packaging technology that they're  working on right now allowing multiple  triplet dies to communicate through an  ultra high bandwidth low latency silicon  interposer which is stacked onto the  package substrate that holds it all  together and frederick orange on twitter  recently pointed to a new die to die  interconnect technology from synopsis  with up to 112 gigabits per second  bandwidth per lane which is something  apple can use to connect two or four  dice together but the point that i'm  trying to make is that the technology  needed to combine multiple dies like  what apple is planning on doing is  already here and in the works but the  best part about going this route is that  every component within each m1 max die  will be put to use so the m1 max quadra  will have eight sets of video encoders  including eight prores encoders and  decoders compared to the current rtx  3090 which still has just a single set  of encoders and decoders it'll also have  16 thunderbolt 4 controllers  256 gigs of unified memory which can be  used as vram and of course 40 cpu cores  but the best part about all of this is  that apple's unified memory architecture  is extremely efficient because the cpu  the gpu and memory are all within the  same soc package which greatly reduces  latency for example matthew and ease  compared the 32 core m1 max gpu and the  new macbook pro to a desktop rtx 3080  gpu and in the only apple silicon  optized game that he tested which was  world of warcraft shadowlands the m1 max  was almost as fast scoring 180 fps  compared to 200 and then translating  that into performance per watt the m1  max completely blows the rtx 3080 out of  the water with almost 7 times the fps  per watt but of course there are barely  any games out there with full apple  silicon support so games that haven't  made the switch are not performing  anywhere close to as well which  definitely gives windows pcs a huge  advantage for gaming but now with all of  that mac stuff out of the way let's jump  into the brand new details we have  concerning the rtx 4000 gpus digi times  recently came out with a report claiming  that nvidia's gaming gpus are getting a  major refresh next year and apparently  it's going to be based on a new ada  lovelace architecture which will be  built on  tsmc's 5 nanometer node which is what  apple's current m1 max chip is built on  and interestingly they mentioned that  nvidia's data center gpus will be built  using tsmc's kowals technology which  means they could be combining dice  together in a package like what apple's  gonna be doing but for the regular rtx  4000 gaming cards nvidia is going to be  sticking to the traditional monolithic  ditec so let's get into what that means  first of all monolithic dyes are massive  just look at this image of the rtx 3090  die which is 628 millimeters squared  literally just by itself compared to the  entire m1 max die which is estimated to  be 429 millimeter squared which also  includes things like the cpu cores the  media engines the thunderbolt  controllers the secure enclave the  memory buses and much more and because  of that the monolithic dye is going to  be more expensive because larger dies  mean lower yields since you can't use up  as much of the edges of a silicone wafer  and if you have fatal flaws within the  wafer or the dyes you have to throw away  a larger portion of it but with apple's  multi-tie method they will have  immensely higher yields since the dies  on the wafer are much smaller and they  can combine them after the fact so it's  going to cost apple significantly less  cash per die and as far as the actual  prices for the rtx 4000 series we  recently got a report that those cards  will be around 10 more expensive because  tsmc recently announced a price increase  for their five nanometer wafers by up to  10  but luckily for apple tsmc is giving  them special treatment by only raising  prices by three percent just for apple  because they're expecting a higher  number of orders but because of the high  10 increase for nvidia some youtubers  like red gaming tech and gamer meld are  expecting the rtx 4090 to cost between  1600 and 1700  compared to the 3090s current msrp of  1500  but of course because of all of the chip  shortages going on including the whole  issue with scalpers reselling gpus the  real price of the rtx 3090 is actually  more than double that with the least  expensive 3090s out there selling for  over 3 000 right now and according to  nvidia ceo the pain isn't going to end  until at least the end of 2022. and to  make things worse there's a new  raptorium cryptocurrency that apparently  works best with amd's cpu processors  instead of using graphics performance  which could potentially cause a huge  shortage of cpus as well but in apple's  case everything is built into one  package and right now they seem to be  immune to the chip shortages because if  you order a 32 core m1 max macbook pro  you can get it before christmas and it's  at the same price as msrp unlike all  those graphics cards and the reason for  that is because apple's chips are  completely scalper proof since they're  built into the max so the point that i'm  making is that if these supply shortages  continue for the next couple of years  apple is going to have a huge advantage  with their m1 max quadra mac pro since  it'll be reusing these m1 max dies that  are already readily available so because  of that we could actually reach a point  where it'll make more sense to buy a mac  to get a seriously high level of  graphics performance instead of trying  to find an rtx 4090 which could be in  short supply and the m1 max quadra will  benefit from having up to 256 gigs of  vram and 8 sets of video encoders which  is currently the biggest limitation for  things like video editing however it  looks like nvidia is going to have the  upper hand in terms of performance  because we recently got new leaks of the  actual core counts that will be built  into the 4090 as high as 18  432 cuda chorus and apparently this  could mean a performance increase of 2.2  times compared to the 3090 which is  absolutely insane and this would mean  that the 4090 would easily outperform  apple's m1 max quadra but don't forget  that they're estimating it to also  consume a lot more power potentially up  to 450 to 650 watts which is  mind-blowingly high and according to  moore's law is dead on youtube going  above 500 watts with the rtx 4090 is  going to be a huge problem because  you're dealing with massive amounts of  heat that will need to be dispersed  properly or else you're going to be  losing all that performance potential to  thermal throttling or diminishing  returns but in apple's case four 32 core  m1 max dies are only expected to use  around 172 watts of power under full gpu  load which is much easier to handle and  cool down but even still the rtx 4090  will likely outperform the best that  apple will have to offer next year which  is very impressive the only question is  how bad are the shortages gonna be and  how high will the real price of this gpu  go thanks to all of the scalpers because  if things continue to go at this rate  buying an apple mac might actually make  more sense if you want a gpu powerhouse  but i guess we're just gonna have to  wait and see so there you guys go if you  enjoyed this video go ahead and click  the circle above to subscribe for more  videos like this one and definitely  check out one of those two right over  there thanks for watching and we'll see  you in the next video  [Music]
 

